sec.t:
लोग हज़ार साल से भी ज़्यादा समय से गाती रेत के बारे में लिखते आए हैं। चीनी लेखकों ने पुराने [[@silk-road|रेशम मार्ग]] पर दुनहुआंग के पास की एक पहाड़ी का ब्योरा दिया, जिसे मिंगशा शान, यानी "गूँजती रेत का पहाड़", कहते हैं। यह आज भी गुनगुनाती है, और सैलानी इसे सुनने ऊँटों पर सवार होकर जाते हैं।

1200 के दशक में इटली के मुसाफ़िर [[@marco-polo|मार्को पोलो]] ने एक ऐसे रेगिस्तान का ब्योरा दिया, जहाँ मुसाफ़िरों को रेत में संगीत, ढोल और आवाज़ें सुनाई देती थीं। उन्हें लगा कि आत्माएँ लोगों को भटकाने की कोशिश कर रही हैं। आज वैज्ञानिक मानते हैं कि शायद उन्होंने गूँजते टीले सुने थे।

{{story:mars-rovers}}

Hydropower uses water falling from a [[@dams-power|dam]] or rushing down a river to spin [[turbine|turbines]]. It is the oldest big source of renewable electricity. The [[@hoover-dam|Hoover Dam]] on the Colorado River has been making power since the 1930s, and India's Himalayan rivers power dozens of projects.

[[geothermal|Geothermal]] energy uses heat from underground. In Iceland, hot water from beneath the ground heats most homes, and steam spins turbines for electricity. It works best in volcanic places, though some heat is available almost anywhere if you dig deep enough.

{{story:solar-panels}}

वोस्तोक स्टेशन [[@antarctica|अंटार्कटिका]] के बीचोबीच बर्फ़ की चादर पर, समुद्र तल से 3 किलोमीटर से भी ज़्यादा ऊँचाई पर है। 21 जुलाई 1983 को, जब वहाँ सर्दी थी, हवा −89.2 °C तक पहुँच गई। इतनी ठंड में साँस लगभग फ़ौरन जमकर क्रिस्टल बन जाती है।

[[sensor|उपग्रहों]] ने पास की बर्फ़ पर इससे भी ठंडी जगहें पकड़ी हैं, लगभग −98 °C के आसपास। पर वे अंतरिक्ष से बर्फ़ की सतह की नापें हैं, [[@thermometer|थर्मामीटर]] से हवा की नहीं, इसलिए उन्हें अलग सूची में रखा जाता है।

{{story:wettest-place}}

The Moon's [[gravity|gravity]] pulls on the oceans, raising two bulges of water on opposite sides of Earth. As Earth turns, coasts pass through them, giving most beaches two high and two low tides a day. The Sun does the same, but more weakly, so the Moon causes about two-thirds of the tidal effect. The shape of the coast matters too. In Canada's Bay of Fundy, the sea can rise and fall by more than 15 metres. In India, the Gulf of Khambhat in Gujarat has some of the country's biggest tides, around 10 metres at their highest.

Tides create a strip of shore that is sometimes underwater and sometimes dry. Creatures in this zone, such as crabs, barnacles and the life of [[@tide-pools|tide pools]], have to cope with both. Some scientists have suggested that this repeated wetting and drying helped early life, or helped sea creatures adapt to land. These ideas are interesting, but they are hard to prove.

{{story:tide-pools}}

Every plane has a balance point, called the centre of [[@gravity-fall|gravity]]. If it is too far back, the plane tips its nose up, slows down and stalls. If it is too far forward, the plane dives. Most paper planes fly best with the balance point a little in front of the middle of the wings. A paper clip on the nose is the easiest fix.

The back edges of the wings act like the elevators on a real plane. Bend them up slightly and the nose lifts. Bend them down and it dives. Bend one up and one down, and the plane rolls into a turn.

{{story:wright-brothers}}

The blue and green of a peacock's feathers are not mostly [[@art-materials|paint-like pigments]]. They come from tiny, regular structures inside the feather that reflect certain colours of light. That is why the colours shift as the bird moves, like a [[@soap-bubbles-colours|soap bubble]]. Scientists call this structural colour. It works differently from mixing paint, as you can see in [[@colour-mix|how colours mix]].

Some animals use [[@camouflage|colour to hide]], like the [[@tiger-stripes|tiger]] in long grass. The peacock does the opposite. Its whole display is made to be seen.

{{story:dino-colors}}

Giant kelp is one of the fastest-growing living things on Earth. In good conditions, a frond can grow about half a metre in a day. Within a few months, a young kelp can reach the surface from the sea floor many metres below.

A grown kelp forest can be as tall as a big forest tree on land. Along the coast of northern California, the [[@redwoods|redwoods]] on land and the giant kelp just offshore are both among the tallest living things in their worlds. Storms tear some kelp loose every winter, but the forest grows back fast as long as conditions are right.

{{story:redwoods}}

Most waves are a few metres high, but some are giants. On 1 January 1995, instruments on the Draupner oil platform in the North Sea measured a single wave about 25.6 m tall, far bigger than the waves around it. Sailors had long told stories of such rogue waves. Many scientists doubted them, until this measurement proved they were real. They can form when several waves pile on top of each other at the same moment.

At Nazaré in Portugal, an underwater canyon focuses the waves of winter storms. Surfers there have ridden waves taller than an eight-storey building.

{{story:tsunami}}

Coastal California is often foggy. Redwoods can soak up [[@fog-smog|fog water]] directly through their needles. This helps the tops of the trees, where pulling water up from the ground is hardest.

Summer fog rolls in from the cold Pacific almost every day, especially near [[@san-francisco|San Francisco]] and further north. Fog dripping off redwood needles can also soak the forest floor, watering ferns and young trees even when it hasn't rained for weeks.

{{story:clouds-rain}}

In the 1970s, two scientists put kangaroos on a treadmill and measured how much oxygen they used. The result surprised everyone. Between gentle hopping and quick bounding, the energy cost barely changed. The kangaroos simply took longer hops instead of hopping more often.

Breathing helps too. As a kangaroo lands, its guts slide forward and push air out of its lungs. As it takes off, they slide back and pull air in. So its breathing rides along with its hops for free.

{{story:springs}}

A typical fridge magnet's field is around 100 times stronger than Earth's own magnetic field, the one that turns a compass. That sounds big until you meet the magnets inside a hospital MRI scanner, which are hundreds of times stronger again and can yank a metal chair across a room.

Fridge magnets are weak on purpose. A tiny neodymium magnet, the silver kind in earbuds, can pinch your skin if two of them snap together. Most fridge magnets would struggle to hold a thick school notebook, which is fine, because their job is a shopping list.

{{story:earth-magnet}}

For a rice farmer in Odisha or Bengal, the year starts with a nervous wait. Seedlings are grown in a small, crowded nursery bed. When enough rain has fallen, the fields are flooded and churned into mud. Then the seedlings are pulled out and replanted by hand, a few at a time, in neat rows.

It is back-bending work, often done by whole families and hired workers together. If the rain comes two weeks late, everything shifts. If it comes too hard, young plants can drown. About half of India's farmland still depends mainly on rain rather than canals or wells, so the weather report matters more here than almost anywhere.

{{story:monsoon}}

There is no single tribal way of life. The Todas of the Nilgiri hills keep buffaloes and live in barrel-shaped huts. The Bhils are famous archers and farmers. The Khasis of Meghalaya trace family names through the mother's side. The Siddis of Gujarat and Karnataka have African ancestors who arrived centuries ago.

Many communities have deep knowledge of forests, plants and animals. Some hold festivals for seeds, rain or harvest. Bonda women in Odisha wear rows of bead necklaces, and Naga tribes of the northeast build tall carved gateposts and morungs, as seen at the [[@india-wonder-hornbill-festival|Hornbill Festival]].

{{story:india-wonder-hornbill-festival}}

In 1854, a terrible outbreak of cholera struck the Soho area of [[@london|London]]. Most people then believed the disease spread through bad air. A doctor named John Snow suspected dirty water instead.

He marked every death on a street map with a small black bar. The bars piled up around one water pump on Broad Street. Snow persuaded local officials to remove the pump's handle, and the outbreak, which was already slowing, faded away. His map is still taught as one of the first great pieces of disease detective work, and it helped push cities towards [[@water-purify|clean water]] and [[@toilets-sewers|proper sewers]].

{{story:climate-change}}

Banks and big shops use note-counting machines. You drop in a stack of notes, and rollers flick them past a [[sensor|sensor]] one at a time, well over a thousand a minute. Many machines also check for fakes by looking at the paper, the ink and special marks that glow under [[ultraviolet|ultraviolet]] light.

Coins are often weighed instead of counted. A bag of a hundred ₹5 coins, for instance, always weighs about the same. If you know one coin's weight, a bag's weight tells you how many coins are inside.

{{story:upi-payments}}

ग्रोथ प्लेटें नन्हे बच्चे के दौर में सबसे ज़्यादा व्यस्त रहती हैं, और फिर किशोरावस्था के [[@body-growth-spurts|लंबाई के उछाल]] में, जब कुछ किशोर एक साल में कई सेंटीमीटर बढ़ जाते हैं। पिट्यूटरी [[gland|ग्रंथि]] से निकलने वाले [[hormone|हॉर्मोन]], और बाद में किशोरावस्था के हॉर्मोन, इस बढ़त को चलाते हैं।

किशोरावस्था के आखिरी सालों तक ग्रोथ प्लेटें पूरी तरह हड्डी बनकर बंद हो जाती हैं। इसके बाद हड्डियाँ और लंबी नहीं हो सकतीं। डॉक्टर बच्चे के हाथ का एक्स-रे देखकर, ग्रोथ प्लेटें कितनी खुली हैं इससे, उसकी “हड्डियों की उम्र” का अंदाज़ा लगा सकते हैं।

{{story:x-rays}}

दुनिया भर में काले और गहरे भूरे बाल सबसे ज़्यादा आम हैं। एशिया, अफ़्रीका, अमेरिका महाद्वीप और यूरोप के बड़े हिस्से में यही आम रंग हैं। गहरे बालों में ढेर सारा यूमेलानिन होता है, जो खोपड़ी को सूरज की [[ultraviolet|पराबैंगनी]] किरणों से बचाने में मदद करता है।

सुनहरे और लाल बाल मुख्य रूप से उत्तरी और पश्चिमी यूरोप में आम हैं, और उन लोगों में जिनके परिवार वहाँ से आए। लाल बाल दुनिया के लगभग 1 या 2 प्रतिशत लोगों के होते हैं, और [[@scotland|स्कॉटलैंड]] और आयरलैंड में सबसे आम हैं। ये आम तौर पर MC1R नाम के एक जीन में बदलाव से होते हैं। आम तौर पर लाल बालों के लिए दो कॉपियाँ चाहिए, माँ-बाप दोनों से एक-एक।

{{story:dna}}

That person was his wife, Bertha Benz. She had helped pay for his work with money from her family. In August 1888, without telling Karl, she set off with their two sons, aged 15 and 13, to visit her mother in Pforzheim, about 106 km away.

On the way, she bought fuel from a chemist's shop, cleared a blocked fuel line with a hatpin and used a garter to insulate a wire, according to the family's story. When the wooden [[@brakes|brakes]] wore down, she asked a shoemaker to fit leather pads. She arrived that evening and sent Karl a telegram. The trip showed that cars could travel real distances, and it gave Benz ideas for improvements, such as an extra low gear for hills.

{{story:cars-engine}}

Until the mid-1900s, the Santa Clara Valley was farmland, covered with fruit trees. People called it "the Valley of Heart's Delight". Every spring, millions of apricot, plum and cherry trees blossomed, and canneries packed fruit for the whole country.

Stanford University, in Palo Alto, helped change that. An engineering professor, Frederick Terman, encouraged his students to start companies nearby instead of moving east. Two of them, Bill Hewlett and David Packard, began in a garage in 1939. In 1951 Stanford opened an industrial park on its land and rented space to technology firms.

{{story:san-francisco}}

The early fighting went badly. Bolívar's first [[republic|republics]] collapsed, and twice he had to flee. In 1815, in exile in Jamaica, he wrote a famous letter setting out his hopes for a free South America. He argued that the colonies had been kept like children, barred from running their own affairs, and that Spain could never win them back by force. He also warned that freedom would be harder to keep than to win.

Help came from an unexpected place. The president of [[@haitian-revolution|Haiti]], Alexandre Pétion, gave him ships, guns and money. Haiti asked one thing in return: free the enslaved people in the lands you win. Bolívar promised, though he kept the promise only in part.

{{story:mountains-tallest}}

Mysore's soldiers used rockets made of iron tubes packed with gunpowder, tied to long bamboo poles or sword blades. They could fly up to around two kilometres. Thousands of rocket men were part of Tipu's army.

After Tipu's defeat, the British shipped captured rockets to England. An inventor named William Congreve studied them and developed the Congreve rocket. Britain fired these rockets at Fort McHenry, in Baltimore, in 1814. Watching the attack, the American lawyer Francis Scott Key wrote of "the rockets' red glare" in the poem that became the US national anthem. A weapon from Mysore had echoed all the way to Maryland.

{{story:rockets-how}}

Dahl was born in Llandaff, near Cardiff in [[@uk-countries|Wales]]. His parents came from Norway, and he spent summer holidays there. His father died when he was three.

At boarding school in England, he was often unhappy. He wrote later about harsh punishments from teachers, and many of his fictional grown-ups seem to come from that time. At Repton School, the [[@chocolate-world|chocolate]] company Cadbury sometimes sent boxes of new chocolate bars for the boys to test. Dahl dreamed of working in a chocolate lab and inventing a famous bar. Decades later, the dream became Willy Wonka's factory.

{{story:chocolate-making}}

A Gujarati dal is thinner than many North Indian dals, with a sweet-sour taste from [[@jaggery|jaggery]] and kokum or lemon. Kadhi, a yogurt curry thickened with gram flour, is made the same way. In Gujarat it's usually a little sweet. In Punjab it's sour and thick.

Some people explain the sweetness through the land. Parts of Gujarat are dry, and the [[@salt-pans|salt marshes]] of the [[@gujarat-rann|Rann of Kutch]] produce much of India's salt. Sweetness balances salt and spice and may help people drink more water in the heat. These ideas are popular, but historians haven't proved any of them.

{{story:gujarat-rann}}
